    "narrative": "The Austin Waves roster is primed for a high-ceiling outing today, headlined by **Jackson Merrill** (75.27) and **Kyle Tucker** (70.00). **Jackson Merrill** is our premier play; he faces a favorable 4.56 xERA matchup against Sasaki and benefits from a 100% stolen base opportunity rate against the starter's slow delivery, making him an elite play in our quest to close the gap in the scarcest category. **Kyle Tucker** remains a foundational force despite a more modest projection, while **Nasim Nunez** (65.68) provides unexpected value; his score is significantly boosted by an 87% stolen base success rate against the slow-delivering Rogers, providing a sneaky path to efficiency even from the #8 spot.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was sidelining **Nolan Schanuel** (53.48), who hovered near the starting threshold all morning. Ultimately, **Nolan Schanuel** was surpassed by the positional flexibility and higher raw efficiency metrics of our middle-of-the-order starters. We are also closely monitoring the platoon dynamics, as **Gunnar Henderson** (58.44) and **Coby Mayo** (57.48) are tasked with overcoming a difficult matchup against Alvarez. While their platoon splits are challenging, the park factors provide a necessary lift to keep them in starting territory. \n\nRisks for this slate center on the bottom of the order, specifically **Josh Lowe** (56.02), who faces a tight window of opportunity against Ginn and is further hampered by an elite defensive catcher who limits the stolen base upside we prioritize. We are proceeding with **Drake Baldwin** (49.03) despite a marginal score, as he represents the best available option at the catcher slot today. We remain vigilant on weather and final lineup confirmations, as any shifts in the batting order for **Sam Antonacci** or **Josh Jung**\u2014both of whom are providing solid value in the #1 and #2 spots respectively\u2014could force a late-afternoon re-evaluation of the bench depth.",
